  }, "1. Introduction: The Autonomic Blind Spot of Modern Mating"), mdx("p", null, "Modern discourse around dating, attraction, and mate selection is saturated with analytical frameworks, yet it remains profoundly blind to the biological substrate that governs its outcomes. On one side of the cultural divide, pop-psychology and mainstream therapy rely on top-down cognitive models. They analyze relationships through the vocabulary of attachment theory, communication scripts, love languages, and conscious compatibility checklists. They operate under the assumption that human beings are rational actors who can identify their relational needs, communicate them clearly, and choose partners based on cognitive alignment."), mdx("p", null, "On the other side, the \"manosphere\" and evolutionary psychology popularizers reduce the mating market to hyper-metricized, transactional games of power and economics. They analyze mate selection using game-theoretic models, status hierarchies, financial metrics, and physical attributes. They generate elaborate, dramatic narratives about \"hypergamy,\" \"sexual market value,\" and \"high-value men.\" While these frameworks sound interesting and generate substantial digital engagement, they suffer from a fatal flaw: they treat the human body as a passive vehicle carrying a computer-like brain that calculates utility."), mdx("p", null, "Both approaches miss the fundamental physiological reality of human connection: ", mdx("strong", {
